                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_need_right:    `<strong>That's the outcome NorthGrid is actually buying.</strong> Behaviour change is what the CHRO sponsor will judge the renewal on — leaders running a decarbonised business differently, not a topic list. Now you can design toward a measurable outcome instead of an elegant agenda nobody renews.`,
                    coach_r1_everything:    `<strong>You're trying to satisfy the sponsor, the MDs and HR at once.</strong> Those are three different programmes — chase all three and the design balloons to ~14 days per learner at an unfundable cost. Name the one outcome renewal is judged on, then decide what is must-have and what is nice-to-have.`,
                    coach_r1_partial:       `<strong>That serves one stakeholder, not the buyer.</strong> Reskilling pleases the divisional MDs and an asset pleases HR, but the renewal is judged on the behaviour change the CHRO sponsor is buying. Lead with the outcome the decision-buyer cares about.`,
                    coach_r1_diag_sharp:    `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> You named behaviour change as the real need AND identified the CHRO sponsor as the decision-economic buyer who signs the renewal. That is the client-first lens — now every design choice has a clear test: does it move the outcome the buyer judges?`,
                    coach_r1_everyone:      `<strong>“Keep everyone happy” has no decision-buyer.</strong> Someone signs the renewal and judges whether it worked — here that's the CHRO sponsor who owns the business outcome. Design for that judgement, not for consensus across contradictory memos.`,
                    coach_r1_topics:        `<strong>Those are topics, not objectives.</strong> “Cover strategy and digital” tells me nothing about what 120 leaders will do differently. Tie each objective to a NorthGrid business outcome you can measure, or you'll build an agenda nobody renews on.`,
                    coach_r2_hybrid:        `<strong>Hybrid is the right instinct.</strong> A custom spine plus modular open components is how you close the £0.8m gap without cutting the outcome — but the spine share matters. Too little custom and fit collapses; too much and the gap barely moves.`,
                    coach_r2_hybrid_balanced: `<strong>That's the architecture.</strong> A 40–60% custom NorthGrid spine for strategy and leadership, with open modules covering generic finance and digital, lands the cost near £1.7m while keeping the relevant content client-specific. Fit and fundability, both protected.`,
                    coach_r2_thinspine:     `<strong>That's cheap-by-default.</strong> A thin custom spine routes most learners through generic open content — the divisional MDs sit in a “Leading Change” module and conclude you didn't listen. You've protected cost and lost the fit that wins the renewal. Which content genuinely must be NorthGrid-specific?`,
                    coach_r2_custom:        `<strong>Beautiful custom design — and it costs £2.6m against £1.8m.</strong> That's an £0.8m hole you can't close by cutting the outcome, and a 16–18-week build that misses the 10-week launch. Where does a hybrid format preserve the impact at a fundable cost?`,
                    coach_r2_open:          `<strong>Cheap and fast — but your fit just dropped to 4/10.</strong> Routing most learners through existing open programmes saves money and loses the renewal. NorthGrid is paying for a bespoke answer to its decarbonisation problem, not a seat in a generic course.`,
                    coach_r2_cutoutcome:    `<strong>You're cutting the outcome NorthGrid is buying.</strong> Dropping objectives until the price fits is exactly the move that loses anchor clients — you close the gap on paper and fail the renewal metric. Close it with format and delivery, not by shrinking the result.`,
                    coach_r2_overbudget:    `<strong>Bidding £2.6m against an £1.8m budget won't fly.</strong> The MD won't approve it and the client can't fund it — you've handed the mandate to a rival. The gap is closed through architecture, not by hoping NorthGrid finds another £0.8m.`,
                    coach_r3_leverage:      `<strong>Faculty as a scarce, high-leverage input — exactly right.</strong> Putting your 3 senior strategy faculty on the 2–3 moments where a professor is irreplaceable, and using practitioners, coaching and digital everywhere else, protects both impact and the £0.6m of open revenue you'd otherwise cannibalise.`,
                    coach_r3_overalloc:     `<strong>You've put your 3 senior faculty into every week — and double-booked them.</strong> Two are committed to open programmes worth £0.6m; spreading them across the whole design cannibalises that revenue and crushes your margin. Where are the 2–3 moments a professor is truly irreplaceable?`,
                    coach_r3_nofaculty:     `<strong>You've protected open revenue by stripping out the faculty.</strong> But the custom strategy spine is exactly where NorthGrid expects senior academic firepower — go too lean and the design loses the credibility that justifies a custom price. Faculty on the leverage points, not nowhere.`,
                    coach_r3_facultyheavy:  `<strong>That's a lot of expensive in-person days.</strong> Beyond a point, more faculty-led intensives add cost and slow the launch without proportionate impact. Shift some load to coaching and digital and reserve in-person for the moments that need the room.`,
                    coach_r3_l1l2:          `<strong>L1/L2 won't earn the renewal.</strong> Happy sheets and knowledge tests measure reaction and learning — but anchor-client renewals hinge on demonstrated behaviour (L3) and business results (L4). Design the L3/L4 measurement in now, not as a bolt-on.`,
                    coach_r3_noimpact:      `<strong>“The relationship will tell us” is how you lose the relationship.</strong> NorthGrid renews on demonstrated impact, not satisfaction. With no measurement designed in, you can't prove the behaviour change the CHRO is buying — and the rival who can will win the renewal.`,
                    coach_r3_configured:    `<strong>That's a deliverable blueprint.</strong> Scarce faculty on the leverage points, L3/L4 impact designed in, and a phased proof cohort that hits the 10-week date — a configuration that protects the outcome, the margin and the timeline at once.`,
                    coach_r3_promise:       `<strong>You've promised 10 weeks on a 16-week build.</strong> You'll launch late or launch under-rehearsed — and with an anchor client a weak first cohort ends the relationship and the reference. Could a phased proof cohort hit the date without betting the mandate?`,
                    coach_r3_slip:          `<strong>Asking NorthGrid to move the date cedes commercial ground.</strong> A 16-week build is safer to deliver, but the client wanted cohort 1 in 10 weeks and a rival may simply say yes. A phased proof cohort usually hits the date without the delivery risk.`,
                    coach_r4_outcomes:      `<strong>Framing on outcomes is what wins anchor mandates.</strong> Leading with what NorthGrid's leaders will do differently — and how you'll prove it — beats a feature list against two rivals. You've made the buyer's business problem the centre of the pitch.`,
                    coach_r4_features:      `<strong>A feature list doesn't differentiate you.</strong> Days, modules and faculty names are table stakes — a rival can match them. Reframe on NorthGrid's business outcome and the impact you'll demonstrate, or you compete on price.`,
                    coach_r4_pricepitch:    `<strong>“Cheapest credible bid” is a race to the bottom.</strong> Lead on price and you invite the discount you're about to be asked for — and you signal the design is interchangeable. Lead on the outcome and the impact guarantee instead.`,
                    coach_r4_trade:         `<strong>Now you're trading, not giving.</strong> Adding days only in return for a year-2 scale agreement turns a margin threat into a relationship win — more commitment for more value, with the margin protected. That's how you concede without destroying value.`,
                    coach_r4_givedays:      `<strong>You just added two days per learner free “to strengthen the offer.”</strong> That's 240 extra learner-days at no fee — your margin fell from 31% to 19%, below the MD's floor. What did you get in return — a longer commitment, a scale agreement? Or did you give value away to look generous?`,
                    coach_r4_phased:        `<strong>A phased cohort-1 proof is the win-protecting move.</strong> Offering a smaller proof commitment that scales after demonstrated impact answers the price pressure without breaking the margin or betting the mandate on an unrehearsed full launch.`,
                    coach_r4_discount:      `<strong>You matched the cut and broke the floor.</strong> Winning the room by accepting the margin hit converts a profitable mandate into a loss-making one — exactly what the MD said he would not approve. Narrow scope or phase the commitment instead of discounting below 30%.`,

